\caption(Actual prior, predicted and corrected scalar state/covariance.)
\alttext(The prior flows through prediction and combines with the observation to produce the captured posterior mean and variance.)

> Derive the linear update and distinguish smoothing

For a fixed linear state/observation model, prediction gives x_minus=F x and
P_minus=F P F transpose+Q. With innovation z−H x_minus, the gain is

$$
K=P^-H^T(HP^-H^T+R)^{-1},\qquad x^+=x^-+K(z-Hx^-).
$$
\alttext(The gain weights the observation innovation using predicted state covariance and observation covariance.)

The native covariance update is the simplified (I−KH)P_minus, using LU inverses;
it is not a Joseph or square-root update. Matrix shape checks do not establish
finite values, positive covariance, independence assumptions or numerical
conditioning. Under a valid linear Gaussian model, the mean is the conditional
MMSE estimate. Floating results remain approximate and can lose covariance
symmetry or PSD.

RTS smoothing uses the whole sequence. Its backward gain is C_i=P_filtered_i F
transpose times inverse(P_predicted_(i+1)), and its mean correction uses the
next smoothed-minus-predicted state. The native smooth() method records one
actual forward pass and one backward pass; no prefix reruns construct its
output. The final smoothed and filtered states agree because no later
observations remain. Conditional covariance reduces in the exact model, but
individual realized errors and one sequence's empirical MSE need not always
decrease.

> Retain the original linear fifty-step study and information form

The original model has dt1, true velocity2, F=[[1,1],[0,1]], H=[1,0], Q=.01I,
measurement variance4, initial state0 and covariance100I. Fifty position-only
observations use mt19937 seed42 and Gaussian SD2. The original nonlinear
sequence follows those draws with the same engine, as described below. Exact
normal-distribution mapping is standard-library dependent. No external dataset
replaces these controlled simulations.

Information form represents Y=P inverse and y=Y x. Its measurement contributions
are additive, but this implementation converts through covariance form during
prediction and uses matrix inverses for reported state/covariance. Algebraic
equivalence does not imply bitwise equality or a measured speed advantage.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kalman_linear.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/filter/kalman_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto s =
        dm::filter::kalman_family_study(dm::filter::kalman_demo_data());
    const auto &f = s.linear.filtered;
    const auto &m = s.linear.smoothed;
    long double min_delta = 1e100L;
    double max_info = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < f.size(); ++i) {
        auto delta = f[i].P;
        for (int r = 0; r < 2; ++r) {
            max_info =
                std::max(max_info, std::fabs(f[i].x[r] - s.information[i].x[r]));
            for (int c = 0; c < 2; ++c)
                delta[r][c] -= m[i].P[r][c];
        }
        min_delta =
            std::min(min_delta, dm::filter::kalman_min_eigenvalue2(delta));
    }
    std::print("| actual method | final position | final velocity | realized position MSE |\n| --- | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n| filtered | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12e} |\n| RTS smoothed | {:.12f} | " "{:.12f} | {:.12e} |\n| information | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12e} |\n",
               f.back().x[0], f.back().x[1],
               dm::filter::kalman_position_mse(f, s.data.linear_truth),
               m.back().x[0], m.back().x[1],
               dm::filter::kalman_position_mse(m, s.data.linear_truth),
               s.information.back().x[0], s.information.back().x[1],
               dm::filter::kalman_position_mse(s.information,
                                               s.data.linear_truth));
    std::print("\nMaximum actual information/KF state difference {:.12e}; minimum symmetric-part eigenvalue " "of filtered-minus-smoothed covariance {:.12e}.\n",
               max_info, min_delta);
    std::print("\n| original step | measurement | true position | actual filtered position | actual " "smoothed position |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i : {0, 1, 2, 9, 24, 49})
        std::print("| {} | {:.12f} | {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", i + 1,
                   s.data.linear_measurements[i][0], s.data.linear_truth[i],
                   f[i].x[0], m[i].x[0]);
    std::vector<double> t(f.size());
    std::iota(t.begin(), t.end(), 1.);
    std::vector<double> z;
    for (const auto &row : s.data.linear_measurements)
        z.push_back(row[0]);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(t, s.data.linear_truth, "truth", {17, 24, 39}, 2).points(
        t, z, "obs", {160, 160, 160}, 3).line(t,
                                              dm::filter::kalman_state_column(
                                              f), "KF", {220, 38, 38}, 2).line(
        t, dm::filter::kalman_state_column(m), "RTS", {5, 150, 105}, 2).title(
        "Original linear sequence: actual KF and RTS states").x_label("timestep").y_label("position").y_limits(-10, 140).save_svg("build/kalman_linear.svg");
}
