> Keep all four nonlinear estimators, including poor outcomes

The original sensor is h(x)=2 position+sin(position), with
derivative2+cos(position) at least one. It is monotonic, avoiding a separate
sign ambiguity, but it still makes a broad uncertain prior non-Gaussian after
observation. The original fifty nonlinear measurements have Gaussian SD.5,
continued from the same seed42 engine, and use R=.25.

EKF uses central-difference Jacobians (native default step1e−5). UKF
propagates2n+1 sigma points; with lambda=alpha²(n+kappa)−n, mean weights are
lambda/(n+lambda) and1/(2(n+lambda)), with the native covariance
correction1−alpha²+beta at the central point. The original defaults are
alpha=.001,beta2,kappa0. Sigma rules are approximations, and large cancelling
weights can be sensitive to approximation and rounding. Neither monotonic
sensing nor this finite experiment guarantees that UKF is better than EKF.

The stochastic EnKF uses300 members and seed7, sample cross-covariance and
independently perturbed observations. The bootstrap particle filter uses300
particles and seed7, Gaussian likelihoods in log-weight form, and unconditional
multinomial resampling after each update. These are finite Monte Carlo
approximations; reporting all complete trajectories is not a universal ranking
of methods.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kalman_nonlinear.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/filter/kalman_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
