> Scope, ownership and verification

The native family helper validates finite matched scalar observations/truth and
complete input-step/population ceilings before copying data or constructing
filters. Defaults admit10000 total original steps and100000 combined
ensemble/particle members; zero disables optional ceilings. These counts exclude
several method output trajectories, temporary matrices, byte/capacity overhead
and peak RAM. The original populations remain300 each with seed7. Underlying
numerical exceptions propagate and no partial family result is returned. Generic
single-step capture follows the mutable filter contract and cannot roll back an
already committed prediction when a later measurement fails.

All displayed trajectories own actual native state/covariance outputs. Model
callbacks remain alive during each native filter call and are not retained in
returned results. KF/RTS uses one native forward/backward run; independent
estimators and parameter controls are separate complete experiments, not prefix
replay. Covariance eigenvalue diagnostics use the symmetric part and ordinary
rounded arithmetic, so they are observations rather than exact PSD proofs.
Plotting never substitutes cached conclusions for computed outputs.

Dense linear filtering costs matrix operations per step, smoothing adds storage
for the complete sequence, and sampled methods additionally scale with their
member/particle counts. The fixed-gain trackers omit covariance adaptation. No
timing benchmark or distribution-free accuracy ranking is inferred from this
study. In particular, a monotonic nonlinear sensor does not make Gaussian
approximations exact, algebraic information equivalence is not bitwise identity,
and a smoother's covariance advantage is not a guarantee about every realized
error.

Independent NumPy forward Kalman and backward RTS recursions verified all 50
linear state/covariance pairs. The original report program was reconstructed and
all original observations, truths, seeds and full family trajectories matched
exactly, including both fixed-gain trackers. The frozen original Kalman header
also produced bitwise-identical KF/RTS trajectories. Native controls cover
scalar exact values, ownership, input/population ceilings and mutation on
capture failure. The shared ten-report build passed 131 native tests, 38
directly instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled
public examples, documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten
representative clangd hovers. All five complete C++ report programs executed
successfully and their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are
retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> References

R. E. Kalman (1960), “A New Approach to Linear Filtering and Prediction
Problems”, Journal of Basic Engineering82(1),35–45,
[doi:10.1115/1.3662552|https://doi.org/10.1115/1.3662552]. H. E. Rauch,F.
Tung,C. T. Striebel (1965), “Maximum Likelihood Estimates of Linear Dynamic
Systems”, AIAA Journal3(8),1445–1450,
[doi:10.2514/3.3166|https://doi.org/10.2514/3.3166].

S. J. Julier,J. K. Uhlmann (2004),
[Unscented Filtering and Nonlinear Estimation|https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~murphyk/Papers/Julier_Uhlmann_mar04.pdf],
Proceedings of the IEEE92(3),401–422. G. Evensen (1994),
[Sequential Data Assimilation with a Nonlinear Quasi-Geostrophic Model Using Monte Carlo Methods to Forecast Error Statistics|https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1029/94jc00572],
JGR99(C5),10143–10162.

The original family background also includes Anderson and Moore's Optimal
Filtering(1979) for information form; Burgers,van Leeuwen,Evensen(1998), Monthly
Weather Review126,1719–1724 for stochastic ensemble analysis;
Gordon,Salmond,Smith(1993), IEE ProceedingsF140,107–113 for bootstrap particle
filtering; and Benedict,Bordner(1962), IRE Transactions on Automatic
Control7(4),27–32 for fixed-gain tracking. The actual native variant details
used here are stated above rather than attributing all implementation choices to
those sources.
